{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the J4 repair at head 45542659c49f5e3fb7c3462a48842e8d6ef3fa54, tree 39c0bfaa, for request 0c3091fa and review request a8f225cd. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThe head adds eleven commits to the reviewed head 77284b97. I read the rewritten implementation note first. Then I read the diff from 77284b97, against the five items of review 8eba6b83, ruling e7962aa7 and ruling 4e199bd8. The source changes are in `packages/derive/src/capability/hold.ts`, `packages/scope/src/workspace-host.ts`, `packages/scope/src/artifacts-host.ts`, the security seals, the docs and the regenerated page module. Two test files are new: `workspace-credential.test.ts` and `workspace-setup-credential.test.ts`. The rest of the head is unchanged, and the findings of 8eba6b83 on the kernel rules, `hold@1`, version dispatch, lineage and pins stand.\n\n## Items of 8eba6b83\n\n1. No test covered the credential route. Resolved.\n   - `workspace-credential.test.ts` runs the real `WorkspaceHost` over its SQL custody, with a stand-in provider. One case refuses a wrong preparing key and keeps the plaintext. It then hands off once, clears the plaintext, and refuses a second read from a new host instance.\n   - A second case refuses a wrong holder, an ended hold, a past instance, a fork that is not selected, and a wrong fork ID or name. Each refusal keeps the plaintext, and the restored state then hands off.\n   - A third case shows the caps: 900 seconds for a workspace token, 120 seconds for fork-read and staging tokens, and the remaining hold time for all three. Fork-read and staging tokens never pass the handoff, and an expired token is refused without its plaintext being erased.\n   - The control at 9a23e92b removes the preparing-key check. The wrong-key case then fails on an ordinary `AssertionError`, and the other two cases pass. The test file and the checked lines are unchanged from 9a23e92b to the gated source.\n2. The custody owner had no security seal. Resolved. `scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs` now seals `workspace-host.ts`, `workspace-lineage.ts` and `native-birth.ts`. The `docs/security/entry-points.md` paragraph names them, and the `scope-index.ts` comment describes its new gate. I reran the file: 10 of 10.\n3. A fork's setup credential could stay live with nothing to revoke it. Resolved under ruling e7962aa7.\n   - Each `hold@2` fork attempt now makes a token record with purpose `setup`, in state `minting`, whose ID names that attempt (`hold.ts`, `setupToken`). The opening makes it when the seed is known. Otherwise the confirmed `head` answer makes it, and each later attempt makes its own.\n   - A confirmed fork answer, selected or not, moves the token to `revoking` and opens the existing revoke in the same outcome. This entry is what the ruling calls \"live and opens its revoke\": the existing hold-end path also moves a live token to `revoking` with its revoke open.\n   - A refused answer ends the token. An unknown answer leaves it `minting`, and a late confirmed answer then opens both the deletion and the revoke.\n   - A confirmed fork answer is well formed only while its attempt has exactly one `minting` setup token. Hold end moves only `live` tokens, so an unknown attempt's token and handle survive the hold's end.\n   - `hold@1` is unaffected, because each new branch checks `v2`. Setup tokens don't count toward the holder's token limit, and they don't block deletion of the selected fork.\n   - On the host side, `workspaceFork` no longer revokes. It stores the plaintext under `workspace:\u003cname\u003e` and reads it back before it confirms. A thrown error keeps the plaintext and gives no answer. The setup branch of `WorkspaceHost.#revoke` checks the token's hold, operation, attempt and opening entry. It also requires the exact attempt name before it calls `revokeCredential`.\n   - `artifacts-host.test.ts` covers the real provider's `revokeCredential` with a `workspace:` handle. An unavailable revoke keeps the plaintext, and a confirmed one clears it. A refused attempt stores nothing.\n   - The native witness `workspace-setup-credential.test.ts` runs native lane genesis, acts, outcomes, ledger and SQL, with labelled stand-ins for ancestry, authority, clock, provider and Git. Its three cases cover the conditions of the ruling:\n     - a revoke that times out leaves the token `revoking`, the plaintext held and the revoke open, and a later confirmed revoke ends the token and clears the plaintext;\n     - a refused attempt leaves no stored credential;\n     - an unknown answer followed by a late confirmation opens both the deletion and the revoke.\n   - The control at the gated source removes the setup revoke opening. The timeout case and the late-confirmation case then fail on ordinary `AssertionError`s, and the refusal case still passes.\n   - `docs/hosts.md` now says that every setup credential the fork adapter issues has a recorded cleanup obligation, with no open limit.\n4. The kernel-gate text did not name all of the additions. Resolved.\n   - Section 7 now names the `hold@2` declaration and the four exports, with a reason for each. It names the `setup` purpose and the fork rule's two added effects.\n   - It states the new bounds: `head` has at most four effects, one operation and closure 78; `fork` has at most five effects, two operations and closure 12. A creating fork reserves 78 in v2 and 42 in v1.\n   - These match the code. The head closure 2·3·(1+6+6) is 78, and the fork closure 6+6 is 12.\n   - `node scripts/check-kernel-boundary.mjs` checks 126 production sources with no refusals.\n5. The manifest did not follow ruling 4e199bd8. Resolved.\n   - The manifest now lists the 337 files the note cites. It names the two collections once each, with file count, byte total and the SHA-256 of their `SHA256SUMS`.\n   - The manifest has SHA-256 4467d7c4 and 36,972 bytes, and `check-notes` gives no warning.\n\n## The gates\n\n- The failed rows stay failed: gate C at 40230449, the continuation at E, gate F at 9d775412 and the diagnostic at F. The follow-ups at R stay their own rows. The repair gate replaces the disposition of ruling 458166da.\n- The repair changed production source, so a new full gate was needed. The gate at 17c14b11, tree 58526e56, passes all five stages. Its receipt has SHA-256 e67f0aa7 and exit 0, with the same head and tree before and after, no timeout and no dropped bytes. Node passes 80 cases, Counting 56, Plain 3,145 with one skip and Native 859 with two skips, with no unhandled error.\n- From 17c14b11 to the head, only the note and the manifest change, which the documents-only rule in `docs/testing.md` covers.\n- The page module was regenerated at 5dc988c8 with the maintained build. From 5dc988c8 to the gated source, no kernel file changes, and the committed `assets.ts` has the build's SHA-256, 310205a1. The gate passes its asset case.\n- The native setup witness failed five times before it passed. Owner 5 exposed a production defect: `WorkspaceHost` passed the bare branch name to `branchRef`, which needs `refs/heads/\u003cbranch\u003e`. Commit 17c14b11 fixes both read sites, and the gate ran after that fix. The failed attempts stay recorded.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 31780874 gives the head tree 39c0bfaa.\n- The request replaces aead7f40 (supersede aa5aa1ac).\n- The 57 artifacts name exactly the 57 changed paths, all at this head, with the note first. Each reads stale only because it rests on question 9873fe58, which the builder retired after reading ruling e7962aa7.\n- I ran the owning files once each, with no whole suite:\n  - plain: `forms-hold-v2`, `forms-hold-dispatch`, `forms-hold`, `floor`, `j4-definitions` and `definitions`: 23 of 23;\n  - native: `workspace-credential`, `workspace-setup-credential`, `artifacts-host`, `workspace-lineage` and `j4-workspace.scope`: 16 of 16;\n  - `security-entry-points.test.mjs`: 10 of 10.\n  - The builder worktree stayed clean.\n- The evidence verifies under ruling 4e199bd8:\n  - all 337 listed files match by size and SHA-256;\n  - the repair collection has 2,002 files and 127,369,064 bytes; its `SHA256SUMS` matches its digest and reruns clean, with no unlisted file and nothing writable;\n  - the original collection still has 1,471 files and 98,061,085 bytes, with root `SHA256SUMS` digest 375a2d00 and nothing writable.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 761 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes from 31780874: 0 warnings.\n- check-commit-messages from 31780874: 17 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. A confirmed fork answer that arrives after the hold closes still leaves the fork `creating`. In v2 that fork now reserves 78 entries for good. The `head` rule's `hold-closed` outcome would fit here too.\n2. v2 still counts a fork creation twice, in the open operation's closure and in the `creating` record. The error is on the safe side.\n3. Refused fork attempts keep opening after the hold ends. Only the host's held check stops the creation calls.\n4. In `workspaceFork`, a name mismatch or an unreadable token in the provider's reply returns before custody, and so does a failed custody write. The fork then has an issued credential with no plaintext held, and its setup token stays `minting` with nothing that can revoke it. Each case needs a provider or storage fault.\n5. A confirmed fork's setup credential now stays live at the provider until the journal's revoke runs, where the reviewed head revoked it inside the call. The plaintext stays in private custody throughout.\n6. The provider's reported expiry still goes into custody without a check against the requested lifetime, and expired plaintext stays in SQL until a revoke confirms. It is never returned.\n7. Fork deletion and selection still accept any valid repository name. Setup revocation does check the exact attempt name.\n8. A mint that the `ready` hook parks waits for the next commit, which affects liveness only.\n9. The note is 40,487 bytes, just under the 40,960-byte limit. It still relies on private shorthand: Root, RAW, P, W, I, C(L) and the letter E for both an empty stream and a commit.\n\n## Architecture\n\nCleanup of a setup credential now lives in the journal, with the existing token and revoke forms. The kernel decides what each fork answer owes, and the host only holds the plaintext and answers the revoke. That removes the second, private cleanup path. `hold@1` stays unchanged beside it. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\nA test now shows each check on the credential route, with a control that fails when the key check is removed. The custody, lineage and birth-decoding owners are sealed. Every setup credential that the provider could have issued has a recorded revoke that the journal owns, and the witness shows that a timed-out revoke stays owed. The remaining gaps need a provider or storage fault (point 4). Approved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe repair reuses the token record, the revoke rule and the existing custody interface, and adds no record kind or host primitive. The fork call no longer revokes, so one revoke path serves both setup and workspace tokens. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks:\n  - The `hold@2` kernel rules, now with setup cleanup, hold under section 7 and rulings adb1a6ed and e7962aa7.\n  - `hold@1` and every existing pin are preserved.\n  - The five items of 8eba6b83 are resolved, each with its test or control.\n  - The repair gate passes at 17c14b11, and every earlier failure stays its own row.\n  - The evidence verifies under 4e199bd8.\n  - The scope stays source readiness under 241248a9. The local scenario uses person members with role agent and labelled provider stand-ins. Live J4 waits for the landing, the deploy and the planner's report.\n\nA landing check will follow the landing. The head changes Worker code: the Scope Worker's workspace host, adapter and wiring, and the regenerated page module. The planner deploys all three Workers after the landing, under ruling 241248a9.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:876f20ad34413c2e0ee5a9388b2ae956c4c508b0","binding":"assigned","head":"45542659c49f5e3fb7c3462a48842e8d6ef3fa54","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c3d5bdfb1d98048de713fd952953ba7438043c7b\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:710449ccd016033f1a6b40da348060ad7fe64c4a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8a112be240c00151934dd994c8c57b199d4b4a39","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0177aa46bbefe761fc9e4045d88b27395f18b586, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:017ed7fa3475275a64ff4632b31319556cf5a96e, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:166e852f3e27145a9b4ccf9ae93b546c02734af8, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:16a7b0d69e598c83634edc7bd2758dec4358b1b4, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:18246e72cbec78736be3f20af842d90465e3e910, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1ad4562485814a95085a0f6314b65d4d9452cbc5, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1f29003b180fddd02d9e23e206a908ed4dbf77a3, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:298ff558b7014a0068089dc5bc81b13607a14fc0, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3b727e4fd602cc10ac9d346ab05d6f8861a38048, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:435f0aee36916ed16e6858e855c2398fa0d5292a, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:457fa70b98632af149ed95de19012cbd66a56f07, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:46ce0b69c4b9dd